{"answer":"The critic's case addresses a different probabilistic model than the one the target invokes, and therefore does not refute the target's claim as stated. The critic's computation of 2^{1-2ℓ} is the probability that a uniformly random coloring of the whole graph happens to realize a stable two-tone cycle. But the target's mechanism does not assert that a uniformly random coloring produces stable cycles; it asserts that the deterministic descent drives the cut boundary onto short cycles and locally equilibrates the tree-like branches so that the third neighbors end up consistent with the cycle's colors with positive constant probability per cycle. Under that model, the per-cycle trapping probability q_ℓ is a property of the dynamics, not of an independent-coloring coin-flip calculation. The critic's 2^{1-2ℓ} figure simply does not apply to the descent's terminal measure, which is heavily conditioned by the dynamics and, plausibly, far more concentrated on two-tone boundaries than a uniform coloring. Consequently, the critic's summation to O(1) establishes only that stable cycles are rare under uniformly random colorings — a fact the target itself already flagged as a countercondition (its first listed countercondition notes the Θ(1)-vs-Θ(log n) question). It does not establish that the descent's terminal states have O(1) expected stable-cycle obstructions, which is the claim actually at issue. Two further points: (1) the critic's premise that expected ℓ-cycles are ~2^ℓ/(2ℓ) — while the more standard statement is that cycle counts in random 3-regular graphs are asymptotically Poisson with mean ~2^(ℓ-1)/ℓ, i.e., exponentially growing in ℓ, not constant mean 1/(2ℓ) — matters here: if cycle counts grow exponentially in ℓ, then even a per-cycle trapping probability decaying only polynomially (or a q_ℓ that does not decay as 2^{-ℓ}) can yield a growing total, so the critic's own cycle-count premise undercuts the force of the 2^{1-2ℓ} bound unless q_ℓ truly decays like 2^{-ℓ} under the dynamics, which the critic has not shown; (2) the target explicitly designates the Θ(1)-per-cycle equilibration probability as its mechanism and proposes a falsifiable simulation (count terminal states containing stable cycles vs n), so the honest status of the dispute is that both the Θ(log n) claim and the critic's O(1) collapse are conditional on an unproven per-cycle trapping probability under the dynamics — the critic has shown the claim is unproven under one model but has not established its falsity under the model the target actually proposes. The consensus-probability-tends-to-0 verdict therefore remains open, not refuted."}